<item>
  <title>Logistic growth</title>
  <link>https://kevanrastello.github.io/resources/logistic-growth.html</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 



<section id="the-problem-with-exponential-growth" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-problem-with-exponential-growth">The problem with exponential growth</h2>
<p>The simplest population model says each individual contributes offspring at a constant per-capita rate <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r">:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7BdN%7D%7Bdt%7D%20=%20rN%0A"></p>
<p>which gives <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N(t)%20=%20N_0%20e%5E%7Brt%7D">. This is a fine description of a population that has just arrived somewhere empty, and a terrible description of one that has been there a while — it predicts unbounded growth forever.</p>
<p>The fix is to let the per-capita growth rate <em>decline</em> as the population fills up. The logistic model makes that decline linear:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7BN%7D%5Cfrac%7BdN%7D%7Bdt%7D%20=%20r%5Cleft(1%20-%20%5Cfrac%7BN%7D%7BK%7D%5Cright)%0A"></p>
<p>Read the right-hand side as the correction term it is. When <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20%5Cll%20K">, the bracket is close to 1 and growth is nearly exponential. When <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20=%20K">, the bracket is zero and growth stops. When <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20%3E%20K">, the bracket is negative and the population declines. The parameter <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?K"> — the <strong>carrying capacity</strong> — is the density at which births exactly balance deaths.</p>
<p>Rearranged, this is the form you’ll usually see:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7BdN%7D%7Bdt%7D%20=%20rN%5Cleft(1%20-%20%5Cfrac%7BN%7D%7BK%7D%5Cright)%0A"></p>
</section>
