<p>Three things are worth noticing as you move the sliders:</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r"> controls the speed, not the destination.</strong> Every trajectory ends at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?K"> regardless of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r"> — a larger <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r"> just gets there sooner. This is why <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?K"> are often described as separable “how fast” and “how much” parameters.</li>
<li><strong>Starting above <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?K"> decays smoothly down to it.</strong> Set <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N_0%20%3E%20K">: there is no overshoot, no oscillation. The continuous-time logistic model simply cannot produce cycles. (Its discrete-time cousin can, and does — that’s a separate resource.)</li>
<li><strong>The inflection sits at exactly <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?K/2">.</strong> Growth is fastest when the population is at half its carrying capacity, which is the origin of the maximum sustainable yield idea in fisheries management.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section id="where-the-growth-rate-peaks" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="where-the-growth-rate-peaks">Where the growth rate peaks</h2>
<p>That third point falls straight out of the model. The growth rate <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?dN/dt"> is a quadratic in <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N">:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7BdN%7D%7Bdt%7D%20=%20rN%20-%20%5Cfrac%7Br%7D%7BK%7DN%5E2%0A"></p>
<p>Differentiate with respect to <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N"> and set to zero:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cfrac%7Bd%7D%7BdN%7D%5Cleft(%5Cfrac%7BdN%7D%7Bdt%7D%5Cright)%20=%20r%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B2r%7D%7BK%7DN%20=%200%0A%5Cquad%20%5CLongrightarrow%20%5Cquad%0AN%5E*%20=%20%5Cfrac%7BK%7D%7B2%7D%0A"></p>
<p>so the maximum growth rate is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r%20K%20/%204">, achieved at half carrying capacity.</p>
</section>
<section id="stability" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="stability">Stability</h2>
<p>The model has two equilibria, where <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?dN/dt%20=%200">: extinction at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20=%200"> and the carrying capacity at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20=%20K">. To classify them, evaluate the derivative of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f(N)%20=%20rN(1%20-%20N/K)">:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Af'(N)%20=%20r%20-%20%5Cfrac%7B2r%7D%7BK%7DN%0A"></p>
<p>At <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20=%200">: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f'(0)%20=%20r%20%3E%200">, so extinction is <strong>unstable</strong> — a small population grows away from zero. At <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N%20=%20K">: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f'(K)%20=%20-r%20%3C%200">, so the carrying capacity is <strong>stable</strong> — perturbations in either direction decay back to it, at rate <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r">.</p>
<p>This is the whole qualitative story of the model, and it’s why the continuous logistic is well-behaved to the point of being boring: one attracting state, approached monotonically from anywhere.</p>
</section>
