<section id="assumptions-worth-remembering" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="assumptions-worth-remembering">Assumptions worth remembering</h2>
<p>The logistic model earns its simplicity by assuming a great deal:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Density dependence is linear.</strong> Real per-capita growth rates often decline non-linearly, and the theta-logistic model exists precisely to relax this.</li>
<li><strong>No time lags.</strong> Individuals respond to current density instantly. Adding a delay to the density term is enough to generate oscillations.</li>
<li><strong>No age or stage structure.</strong> Everyone reproduces identically, regardless of age or size.</li>
<li><strong>No stochasticity.</strong> Both environmental variation and demographic noise are absent, which matters enormously at small <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?N">.</li>
<li><strong>Closed population.</strong> No immigration or emigration.</li>
<li><strong>Constant environment.</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?K"> don’t vary through time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these is a door to a more realistic model, and most of them are worth walking through only when the data demand it.</p>
</section>
